                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Cave Runner is a game that is not worth the grind. \n\nIt has a lackluster story, no settings for adjusting or muting the volume and graphics as well as unforgiving gameplay mechanics. The first issue I had when playing is the time given per level; in all of them you [i]must[/i] rush to attain the objective or its GG. There’s also bonus Seconds that you can get in the map, but even then, all of them is nearly impossible to beat unless you know what you’re doing. Even then, that’ll take you a good 10+ tries on each level. Another problem is the crappy hitbox. Ever feel enraged, when you see your character die even though they didn’t touch the walls? You’ll be having many of these moments when playing. It’s like the developers slapped a square or circle over the character as the hitbox, instead of reshaping it to meet the figure of the character. So anything that touches the said shape will cause the game to re-start, hence why it appears that our character is dying just being near the walls rather than actually flying into it.\n\nThe controls are even worse. Whichever direction you chose to move, your character will go towards that direction, until you decide to move another way. This is what caused me to die a lot, because of the lack of control there was in being able to stop.\n\nThe last aspect that I disliked was the repetitiveness of the object. Through levels 1 to 50, all you’re doing is collecting a match and returning to the lantern within the [unfair] time limit. Its music is even more monotonous, and becomes agitating over time.\n\nThe art style of the game isn’t anything too interesting. All of it consists of flat, 2D colors with little to no existent gradients or shades. There are no effects or animations for when you spawn, die or complete a level either.\n\nThat being said, Cave Runners is a game that I do not recommend playing.",
